<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gustav I of Sweden</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1496">1496</a>–<a href="/years/1560">1560</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Gustav Vasa · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-vasa">House of Vasa</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gustav I, known as Gustav Vasa, was a Swedish nobleman who led the revolt against Christian II after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stockholm-bloodbath">Stockholm Bloodbath</a> of 1520 and was elected king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> in 1523, ending the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalmar-union">Kalmar Union</a>. He broke with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, confiscated church property and introduced the Lutheran <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a>, and in 1544 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/riksdag">Riksdag</a> made the crown hereditary in his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-vasa">Vasa dynasty</a>. In 1556 he granted the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a> to his son John.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gustav-i-of-sweden.jpg" alt="Gustav I of Sweden" width="312" height="408" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jacob Binck · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gustav_Vasa.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Gustav Eriksson was probably born in 1496, the son of Cecilia Månsdotter Eka and Erik Johansson Vasa, most likely at Rydboholm Castle. He and his father supported the party of Sten Sture the Younger, regent from 1512, against King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christian-ii-of-denmark">Christian II of Denmark</a> in the struggle over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalmar-union">Kalmar Union</a>. Taken hostage by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danes">Danes</a>, Gustav escaped from Kalø in 1519 and reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a> on 30 September, perhaps disguised as a drover. Returning to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> on 31 May 1520, he declined an invitation to Christian's coronation in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stockholm">Stockholm</a>. Close to 100 people, among them his father, were then executed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stockholm-bloodbath">Stockholm Bloodbath</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Gustav went to Dalarna, where the stories of his adventures rest largely on Peder Svart's strongly biased chronicle and cannot be verified. After first being refused, he was appointed military commander, and in February 1521 his force of about 400 men came mainly from the Lake Siljan area. A rebel victory at Brunnbäck's Ferry in April and the sacking of Västerås, with its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> mines, brought him resources and supporters. In August the leading noblemen of Götaland declared him regent at Vadstena. Lübeck joined the war in 1522.</p><p>At Strängnäs in June 1523 the leading men of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> chose him as king, and on 17 June the rebels entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stockholm">Stockholm</a>. The Treaty of Malmö of 1524 made Denmark-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> acknowledge Swedish independence, though Gustav failed to win Gotland and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/blekinge">Blekinge</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>When the Pope refused to accept Gustav's choice of archbishop and demanded the reinstatement of the exiled Gustav Trolle, Gustav appointed Laurentius Petri himself in 1531, and the Pope lost any influence over the Swedish Church. Olaus Petri's New Testament appeared in 1526, and a full <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a>, the Gustav Vasa Bible, in 1540–41. The Church's assets were nationalised and the Lutheran Church placed under his control. He replaced the prerogatives of local landowners, nobles and clergy with centrally appointed governors and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a>, and began a standing army and navy.</p><p>He met resistance: Dalarna rebelled three times in the first ten years, and peasants in Småland under Nils Dacke rose in 1542 over taxes, church reform and the confiscation of church bells, until Gustav defeated them in the spring of 1543 with German mercenaries. He fought an inconclusive war with Ivan the Terrible of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in 1554 to 1557. He summoned representatives of the four estates, including peasants, to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/riksdag">Riksdag</a> assemblies of 1527 and 1544.</p><p>Gustav died on 29 September 1560 and was buried in Uppsala Cathedral. Historians note the brutality of his methods, and the stories of his adventures are considered largely legend and his own propaganda. The annual Vasaloppet ski event and the founding of Helsinki in 1550 are linked with his name.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gustav Vasa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…of the three Scandinavian crowns formed in 1397 under Queen Margaret I. In 1523 Sweden broke away under <b>Gustav Vasa</b>, and in the same year the Danish nobility deposed the Union king Christian II and elected his uncle, Duke Frederick of Schleswig-Holstein, as Frederick I; Norway accepted him…</blockquote><a href="/empire/denmark-norway">Denmark-Norway · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Politically it influenced the Scandinavian kingdoms: the Peace of Stralsund marked the high point of its power over Denmark, and Lübeck’s support helped <b>Gustav Vasa</b> become king of Sweden in 1523.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Gustav Vasa</b>’s Riksdag of Västerås in 1527 began the transfer of church wealth to the crown and the making of a Lutheran state.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-sweden">Kingdom of Sweden · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Gustav I of Sweden</b> claimed all Sámi as Swedish subjects.</blockquote><a href="/culture/sami">Sámi · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>On Badelundaåsen, northeast of Västerås, Swedish troops led by <b>Gustav Vasa</b> defeated a Danish force under Didrik Slagheck and took the town.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-29">29 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1556 <b>Gustav Vasa</b> granted his second son John the Duchy of Finland, covering south-western Finland, as an appanage.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-finland">Duchy of Finland</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Lindholmens, in what is now <a href="/countries/sweden">Sweden</a>. Died in Tre Kronor Castle, in what is now <a href="/countries/sweden">Sweden</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=59.58,18.13&amp;year=1537" aria-label="Lindholmens on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4931 164 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Gustav I of Sweden"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5381 434h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5381 434h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=59.58,18.13&amp;year=1537">What was here in 1537? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
